\section*{Long-Time Existence of the Calabi Flow on Compact Kaehler Manifolds}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Finite-time Calabi-flow singularity}: The manuscript claims a smooth Kähler metric in the standard class on $\mathbb {CP}^{10}$ whose scalar curvature becomes unbounded during Calabi evolution before a finite time. Thus the failure can occur in a class that also contains a constant-scalar-curvature metric.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Finite-time Calabi-flow singularity}: A radial momentum ansatz reduces the geometric flow to a fourth-order scalar equation with smooth endpoint conditions. An exact shrinking solution of the flat model is constructed first. A correction on the compact projective manifold preserves the shrinking mechanism and the positivity of the metric, giving the finite-time curvature singularity.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Finite-time Calabi-flow singularity}: The initial smooth form is in the Fubini-Study class on projective ten-space, which contains a Kahler-Einstein and hence constant-scalar-curvature metric. Its finite maximal smooth time contradicts the required global existence, already enough to negate the conjunction with smooth convergence.

The claimed counterexample lies within the question’s hypotheses and contradicts its universal conclusion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
\textit{Finite-time Calabi-flow singularity}: The pinned catalogue lists no Lean formalization for this result.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

